• Creational patterns:
    ◦ Focus on how objects are created
    ◦ Decouples object creation from object usage
    ◦ Provides flexibility in deciding which object is needed for a given use case
    ◦ Makes the system independent of how objects are created, composed and used
    ◦ Abstracts the complexity of the object request
        ▪ Tell the system what you need, don’t care how its done, just tell it what you want

Intent
    • Provide a way to create objects using a common interface without exposing the creation logic
    • Allows the actual object type to be decided at runtime by a subclass or factory method
Problem
    • When you have multiple related classes, you want to localise/centralise the object creation
Solution
    • Use a factory class with a method that decides which object to create based on input
Benefits
    • Centralises object creation
    • Simplifies client code
    • Easy to add new types down the road